      "text": "VISUAL DIRECTION: Risograph (Two inks, real paper)\nColor strategy: Two or three spot inks overprinting on paper; no pure black\nTypography: A bold grotesk display set big like a flyer, a mono for captions and labels, occasional rotated text.\nColor: Paper ground with two or three inks (for example fluorescent pink and federal blue). Overlaps use mix-blend-multiply. Text is the darkest ink, never pure black.\nLayout and composition: Poster-like stacking and collage: big headline, ticket stubs with dashed perforations, circular stamps, a 2–3px misregistered duplicate on one headline.\nComponents: Ink-filled buttons with a 3px offset shadow in the second ink; ticket-shaped cards; boxed mono labels; stamp badges.\nImagery: Duotone images in the two inks with grain or halftone.\nMotion: Paper-like: quick slides and slight rotation; stamps that thunk in.\nSignature moves to choose from: A misregistered headline; Ticket stubs with perforations; Duotone imagery in the inks.\nNever: No more than three inks; No smooth gradients; No full-color photos; No blurred shadows.\nThe cliché rendition to avoid: Random retro fonts with a beige background. Riso is two inks with discipline."
